{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And you shall cease from leaving him; you shall help him with it.</b> This tells you that specifically when he wants to be with you in his labor and wants to load up together with you — then you are obligated to assist him. But if he sits by himself and says, 'Since the matter is incumbent upon you, you are obligated to load up alone' — therefore it says 'and you shall cease from leaving him,' for the word לוֹ ('him') does not carry in its meaning עִמּוֹ ('with him'), for you are permitted to cease from helping him when he does not want to be with you in his labor. And from here comes a response regarding some of the poor among our people who cast themselves upon the public and refuse to do any work, even though they are able to do some trade or some other thing by which they could bring a morsel to still the hunger of their household, and they pick quarrels if they are not given sufficient for their needs. For this thing HaShem did not command, but only 'you shall help him with it' — 'you shall raise them up with him' (Exodus 23:5; Deuteronomy 22:4): the poor man must do everything he finds in his power to do, and if despite this his hand cannot reach far enough, then every man of Israel is obligated to sustain him and strengthen him and give him sufficient for his need, whatever he lacks — and you shall help even up to a hundred times."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:22:28.303209121Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}